<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Tod</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1782">1782</a>–<a href="/years/1835">1835</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Tod was an officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and an orientalist who served as political agent for the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, from 1818 to 1822. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-and-antiquities-of-rajasthan">Annals and Antiquities of Rajasthan</a> (1829–1832) drew on bardic chronicles and inscriptions to present a romantic history of the Rajputs that influenced both British policy and later Indian nationalist memory, especially of Mewar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-tod.jpg" alt="James Tod" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Painting contemporaneous (Tod died 1830s). Photograph taken before 1920 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colonel_James_Tod.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
